  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Assessing the Vulnerabilities of Migrant Domestic Workers in the Kingdom of Bahrain</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Driven by increasing child and elder care needs, and underpinned by regional demographics and cultural norms, demand for domestic workers is set to continue growing in Bahrain. However, and despite continued progress, as their numbers continue to increase, migrant domestic workers are yet to be fully included in the country&#x2019;s most progressive labour reforms &#x2013; such as, for example, wage protection, internal labour mobility and the flexi-work permit scheme. Responding to wide-ranging efforts by the Government of Bahrain to enhance the protection of migrant domestic workers in the Kingdom, this project seeks to leverage multistakeholder efforts to identify key points of vulnerability and propose targeted recommendations. Informed by key international frameworks, such as Convention No. 189 and Recommendation No. 201 (concerning decent work for domestic workers), this project aims to inform reform efforts by assessing the range of vulnerabilities faced by migrant domestic workers in Bahrain. Based on a comprehensive understanding of the systemic factors faced by migrant domestic workers that can render them vulnerable to exploitation and abuse, a clear set of tailored recommendations for priority areas of work will be developed, providing a key resource in informing evidence-based policy and future interventions.</narrative>
  </description>
